# cut: what the references do differently and the standard leaves open or
# decides the other way.
#  - macOS refuses a byte that is no character ("Illegal byte sequence"), keeps a
#    last line without newline as it is (the standard says "%s\n"), takes a range
#    that runs backwards (5-2) and prints nothing for it, takes -b/-c/-f twice, and
#    -n moves the start of a range back but the end forward, so it selects a
#    character the standard drops. A directory among the files ends it quietly.
#  - GNU does not read -n the way the standard does (its -b 1,3,5 -n on a
#    three-byte character selects nothing, the standard selects the character for
#    the element 3), accepts -n with -c and -f (a usage error here), accepts
#    -d '', and stops at a number it cannot count (we take it as no end).
#  - a number too big to count (99999999999999999999) is an error elsewhere and
#    no end of the line here.
--help
-b -2 -n bad
-b -3 -n bad
-b -4 -n bad
-b 1 -b 2 f
-b 1 -c 1 f
-b 1 -n bad
-b 1 dir f
-b 1,2 -n bad
-b 1,3,5 -n bad
-b 1,3,5 -n euro
-b 1,3,5 -n u
-b 1,3,5 -n uc
-b 1-2 -n bad
-b 1-3 -n bad
-b 1-5 -n bad
-b 1-6 -n bad
-b 1-99999999999999999999 f
-b 18446744073709551616- f
-b 2 -n bad
-b 2 -n euro
-b 2 -n u
-b 2,4 -n bad
-b 2-3 -n bad
-b 2-4 -n bad
